---
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';
import Film from '@/components/Film.astro';
import { local } from '@/lib/paths';
---
<Base title="Flow lab" description="A standalone Python numerical flow experiment with a reproducible scientific film, separate from doctoral research.">
  <article class="case-study">
    <header class="case-header"><a class="back-link" href={local('#projects')}>← Back to selected work</a><p class="eyebrow">Numerical methods / Python / Scientific visualisation</p><h1>Fluid dynamics,<br />made visible.</h1><p class="case-deck">Simple geometry. Rich dynamics. A standalone numerical experiment turns a flow field into a visual account of evolving structure.</p><dl class="case-meta"><div><dt>Context</dt><dd>Independent portfolio demonstration</dd></div><div><dt>Focus</dt><dd>Computation & scientific visualisation</dd></div><div><dt>Deliverable</dt><dd>Reproducible source & a scientific film</dd></div></dl></header>
    <Film name="flow" title="A computational flow experiment" description="Standalone numerical demonstration. This film is not a result from my doctoral research." large />
    <div class="case-columns"><aside class="case-aside"><span class="eyebrow">The question</span><p>How does structure emerge from a moving fluid?</p><a class="text-link" href={local('downloads/flow-study.zip')}>Download the reproducible study ↓</a></aside><div class="case-body"><h2>Make the computation inspectable</h2><p>A two-dimensional D2Q9 lattice-Boltzmann calculation resolves flow past a cylinder at nominal Reynolds number 120. Python and Numba advance the populations on a 672 × 224 lattice. The film follows the saved field snapshots, not an analytically prescribed vortex animation.</p><h2>Show what the field actually contains</h2><p>Cyan marks clockwise vorticity and coral marks counterclockwise vorticity. A fixed, symmetric colour range makes the changing wake comparable through time; values outside the range are clipped only for display. The trace below the field shows transverse velocity at the marked downstream probe.</p><h2>Check the numerical behaviour</h2><p>The implementation checks include equilibrium moments, periodic mass conservation and viscous shear-wave decay. The recorded fields remain finite and the mature wake advects downstream. These checks establish internal consistency, not experimental validation.</p><h2>Keep the scope clear</h2><p>This is a purpose-built portfolio demonstration. It illustrates a computational workflow, not a validated engineering prediction. It is separate from my OpenFOAM research. The modest-resolution model uses a stair-stepped cylinder, periodic transverse boundaries and an approximate outlet; it has not undergone a grid-convergence or experimental-validation study.</p><p>My professional CFD work involves multiphase flow, RANS and LES, and comparison with laboratory measurements. The published studies below provide the research context for that experience.</p><a class="text-link" href={local('#publications')}>Read the published research →</a></div></div>
    <div class="next-project"><span class="eyebrow">Another way to learn a system</span><a href={local('work/black-box-optimisation/')}>Black-box optimisation <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div>
  </article>
</Base>
